      "text": "To solve for \\(a + b\\) given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) and the functions \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we start by substituting \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\).\n\nFirst, express \\(g(f(x))\\):\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5\n\\]\nWe are given that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Therefore, we can set the expressions equal to each other:\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n\\]\nFor these two expressions to be equal for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms must be equal. This gives us two equations:\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2b - 5 = 4\n\\]\nSolving the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\nSolving the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
