      "text": "To solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in the function \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) given the conditions \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):**\n   Given \\(f(x) = ax + b\\), we substitute \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g\\):\n   \\[\n   g(f(x)) = g(ax + b)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   g(ax + b) = 2ax + 2b - 5\n   \\]\n\n2. **Set \\(g(f(x))\\) equal to the given function \\(3x + 4\\):**\n   We are given that:\n   \\[\n   g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\n   \\]\n   Therefore, we equate the two expressions:\n   \\[\n   2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n   \\]\n\n3. **Equate the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms:**\n   By comparing the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms on both sides of the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   2a = 3 \\quad \\text{(coefficient of } x\\text{)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2b - 5 = 4 \\quad \\text{(constant term)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):**\n   From the equation \\(2a = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From the equation \\(2b - 5 = 4\\):\n   \\[\n   2b = 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{9}{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find \\(a + b\\):**\n   \\[\n   a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
